1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,000
Well, welcome back.

2
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,000
Cloud Code gives you several ways to shape how it works,

3
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:06,000
and three of them matter more than the rest.

4
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:09,000
Rules, skills, and slash commands.

5
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:14,000
We'll walk through what each one is, how it loads into context,

6
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:18,000
when to reach for it, and the mix-up people make most often.

7
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:21,000
Let's lock down three terms, because the whole lesson

8
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:25,000
turns on keeping them straight. First, rules.

9
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:27,000
Those are standing, always-on instructions

10
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:31,000
that live in cloud.md and load into context

11
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:32,000
when your session starts.

12
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:37,000
They apply to every turn, and you never call them up. Second, skills.

13
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:43,000
A skill is a reusable capability packaged as a skill.md file

14
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:44,000
with YAML front matter.

15
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:47,000
And Cloud loads it only when your current task

16
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:48,000
matches its description.

17
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:51,000
Third, slash commands.

18
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:54,000
Those are the shortcuts you type with a slash.

19
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:57,000
Some ship built into the tool, and some you write yourself.

20
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Rules, skills, commands.

21
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,000
Keep those three straight, and everything else in this lesson

22
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:05,000
falls into place.

23
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:08,000
Now, here's the key distinction in this whole area,

24
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:10,000
and it's the one people get backwards.

25
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:13,000
Rules and skills load completely differently.

26
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:15,000
A rule is always-on.

27
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:19,000
It loads into context the moment your session starts,

28
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:21,000
and it stays there for every turn.

29
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:22,000
You never invoke it.

30
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:24,000
A skill is the opposite.

31
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:27,000
Only its description sits in context,

32
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:30,000
just enough for Cloud to know the skill exists.

33
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:33,000
The body, all the actual instructions,

34
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:36,000
loads only when you or the model invoke it.

35
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:37,000
That's what makes skills cheap.

36
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:39,000
You can write a long, detailed skill,

37
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:44,000
and it costs you almost nothing until the moment it's actually needed.

38
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:46,000
Always-on versus on-demand.

39
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:49,000
That's the line to remember.

40
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:50,000
Let's stay on rules for a second,

41
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:53,000
because this is where the classic mix-up happens.

42
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:55,000
People assume a rule works like a skill, something

43
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:58,000
you trigger on purpose. It doesn't.

44
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:01,000
A rule loads on its own, either when your session starts

45
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:05,000
or when you touch files whose paths it's scoped to.

46
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:08,000
You never type a slash to fire a rule.

47
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:09,000
And here's the other half of it.

48
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:13,000
As your Cloud.md grows, you can split it

49
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:16,000
into separate topic files, one per subject,

50
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:18,000
so it stays readable.

51
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:19,000
One more thing worth knowing.

52
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:22,000
Rules are context, not enforcement.

53
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:23,000
They shape what Cloud tends to do,

54
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:25,000
but they don't guarantee it.

55
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:28,000
If you need a hard block, that's a job for permissions,

56
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:31,000
or hooks, not a rule.

57
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:34,000
Let's open up a skill and see what's inside,

58
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:36,000
because it's simpler than it sounds.

59
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:38,000
A skill is really just a small folder

60
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:41,000
that Cloud can pick up when it's relevant.

61
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:45,000
Inside that folder is a file called skill.md,

62
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:47,000
and that file holds the instructions.

63
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:50,000
At the top of it sits YAML front matter,

64
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:53,000
and the key piece there is the description.

65
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:55,000
That description is what Cloud reads

66
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:00,000
to decide whether the skill fits the task in front of it.

67
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:02,000
Now here's a detail people trip on.

68
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:05,000
The command name comes from the folder name,

69
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:07,000
not from any field inside the file.

70
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:09,000
So if the folder is called deploy,

71
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:13,000
you invoke it by typing slash deploy.

72
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:15,000
The one thing you really need is a clear description,

73
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:17,000
so the model can find it.

74
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:20,000
This is the mistake I see most often,

75
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:22,000
so let's call it out directly.

76
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:24,000
Someone writes a bare markdown file,

77
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:26,000
drops in some instructions,

78
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:29,000
and expects it to fire reliably as a skill.

79
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:30,000
Here's the nuance.

80
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:34,000
The front matter fields are technically optional.

81
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:36,000
The name defaults to the folder name,

82
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:40,000
and the description falls back to the file's first paragraph.

83
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:44,000
But a skill with no clear description matches poorly,

84
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:47,000
so it fires unreliably, if at all.

85
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:49,000
The description is doing the real work,

86
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:51,000
because that's what the model matches against

87
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:54,000
to decide the skill is relevant.

88
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:57,000
A weak description means almost nothing to match,

89
00:03:57,000 --> 00:03:59,000
so always write one.

90
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:01,000
And placement matters too.

91
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:04,000
The file has to live in a skills or commands folder

92
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:06,000
that Cloud actually scans.

93
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:08,000
A note sitting anywhere else is just a note.

94
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:12,000
Here's a point that clears up a lot of confusion,

95
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:15,000
especially if you learned this from older tutorials.

96
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:18,000
You'll often see slash commands and skills

97
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:21,000
described as two separate features.

98
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:25,000
In current Cloud code, they were merged into one system.

99
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:26,000
Watch how this works.

100
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:29,000
A file at .cloud commands name.md

101
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:32,000
creates a command you invoke as slash name.

102
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:36,000
A skill folder at .cloud skills name

103
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:40,000
with a skill.md inside creates the very same slash name.

104
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:42,000
Both run the same way.

105
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:46,000
So a custom slash command really is just a skill

106
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:48,000
in a slightly different shape.

107
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:50,000
The older commands form still works,

108
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:54,000
but skills are the recommended path now.

109
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:56,000
And if a skill and a command ever share a name,

110
00:04:56,000 --> 00:04:58,000
the skill is the one that wins.

111
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:02,000
So let's split slash commands into two families,

112
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:04,000
because they're not all the same thing.

113
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:07,000
The first family is built in commands.

114
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:09,000
These are coded right into the tool,

115
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:12,000
and they run fixed logic.

116
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:17,000
Think of slash init, slash clear, slash help, slash model.

117
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:19,000
You can't edit what they do.

118
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:21,000
They just work straight out of the box.

119
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:25,000
The second family is custom commands, and these are yours.

120
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:27,000
You write a markdown file,

121
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:29,000
and the file name becomes the keyword

122
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:31,000
you type after the slash.

123
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:32,000
Whatever you put in that file

124
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:35,000
is what the command tells Cloud to do.

125
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:38,000
So built in commands are the tool's own behavior,

126
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:41,000
and custom commands are behavior you author.

127
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:44,000
Same slash, two very different sources.

128
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:48,000
Last piece for this lesson, and it's a practical one.

129
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:50,000
When you create a custom command,

130
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:53,000
where you save the file decides who can use it.

131
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:56,000
There's no registration step, no config to flip.

132
00:05:56,000 --> 00:05:59,000
Put the file under .cloud in your project,

133
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:00,000
and it ships with the repo,

134
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:03,000
so your whole team gets it once they pull.

135
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:06,000
Put it under the Cloud folder in your home directory instead

136
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:09,000
and it's personal, available across every project

137
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:12,000
you work on, but only for you.

138
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:13,000
That's the whole rule.

139
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:16,000
The file's location is what sets the scope.

140
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:19,000
And because the project version lives in version control,

141
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:22,000
checking it in is how you make sure everyone on the team

142
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:25,000
is working with the exact same command.

143
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:28,000
Rules and skills can sound similar,

144
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:31,000
but they load in opposite ways.

145
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:34,000
A rule is always on, applied every turn.

146
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:37,000
A skill loads only when its description matches the task.

147
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:40,000
And a custom command is really just a skill

148
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:41,000
you invoke by name.

149
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:45,000
That covers rules, skills, and slash commands.

150
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:47,000
Take a breath here, and I'll see you in the next one.

